    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(65, 14), (8, 8), (61, 6), (89, 20), (20, 29)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 25.0, 41.0, 21.0]\n    generator_capacities = [57.0, 92.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 300  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220  # kV\n    line_capacity = 50  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 81657  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 34  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Memo: Planning model for a generator-to-consumer transmission build-out\n\nWe are seeking a practical, physics-aware design for a new transmission network that connects our generators to our consumers across the service area. The nodes we intend to connect are located at map coordinates Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (65, 14)\n  - N1 (generator): (8, 8)\n  - N2 (consumer): (61, 6)\n  - N3 (consumer): (89, 20)\n  - N4 (consumer): (20, 29), and each node\u2019s role in the system is specified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Consumer demand levels are defined in Consumer demands: N2: 25.0 MW, N3: 41.0 MW, N4: 21.0 MW, and generator output limits are provided in Generator capacities: N0: 57.0 MW, N1: 92.0 MW. Our task is to decide which transmission lines to construct between these nodes and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met within generator limits, subject to the constraints that power can only flow along lines we choose to build and no line is loaded beyond its rating, which is given in 50 MW.\n\nConstruction costs are distance-based. For any candidate line, the build cost scales with its length computed from the node coordinates at a rate of $81,657/km. Distance therefore affects both capital spending and, as described below, ongoing losses.\n\nOperational costs arise from resistive heating in the conductors. We plan to use copper, with the wire material specified in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper) and the conductor sizing given by 300 mm\u00b2. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross-sectional area; longer, thinner lines are more resistive and therefore more expensive to operate. Our planned operating voltage is 220 kV, which matters because current is proportional to power divided by voltage. Resistive losses follow a square law: loss scales with resistance times the square of the current. In practical terms, that means the loss on a line grows with the square of the power flow; if we double the power flow on a route, we incur roughly four times the resistive heating cost. Because current falls as voltage rises, there is a strong 1/voltage\u00b2 benefit: higher voltage significantly reduces these losses. The cost we attach to these losses is set by $34/MWh, applied to the physics-based loss term of resistance multiplied by the square of power divided by operating voltage (Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)\u00b2). We will not use linear approximations or simply sum flows for operating cost; the square-law behavior must be captured faithfully to reflect the real economics of heat dissipation in copper conductors.\n\nThis is a planning model: power flow is treated as conserved at the nodes. All power injected by generators is accounted for in deliveries to consumers; losses do not reduce delivered energy in the nodal balance. Instead, we assess the financial penalty of those losses separately using the loss cost rate. In effect, adding a line reduces flow on other paths (and therefore their quadratic losses) but also increases our capital outlay; conversely, concentrating flow on fewer lines saves on construction but amplifies heating costs.\n\nThe objective is to minimize total lifecycle cost across the candidate topology: capital construction cost for the lines we build, plus the operational cost of resistive losses under expected power flows. The inputs listed above\u2014locations Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (65, 14)\n  - N1 (generator): (8, 8)\n  - N2 (consumer): (61, 6)\n  - N3 (consumer): (89, 20)\n  - N4 (consumer): (20, 29), node roles ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer'], demands Consumer demands: N2: 25.0 MW, N3: 41.0 MW, N4: 21.0 MW, generator capabilities Generator capacities: N0: 57.0 MW, N1: 92.0 MW, copper properties 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), conductor sizing 300 mm\u00b2, voltage level 220 kV, line ratings 50 MW, build-rate $81,657/km, and loss-rate $34/MWh\u2014provide the data needed to evaluate alternative configurations and select a network that reliably serves load while balancing the trade-off between constructing additional corridors and incurring higher quadratic heating costs on the lines we do build.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning approach for a cost-minimizing transmission build between generation sites and customer load\n\nOur utility is preparing a build-out to link generation to customer load across a compact service area. The candidate sites are located at known map positions, provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}. Each node is designated as either a generator or a consumer in {NODE_TYPES}. Customer requirements are captured in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and generator output limits are given in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nThe key decision is which transmission lines to construct between nodes. Power may only flow over lines we choose to build. The capital cost of any line should reflect its length, so distance between nodes drives the construction budget using {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. We will operate the network at a fixed system voltage and cap each built span\u2019s transfer level to a maximum to respect thermal and reliability constraints; the allowable per-line transfer is specified by {LINE_CAPACITY}.\n\nOperating costs are dominated by resistive heating in the conductor. We are using copper, and the wire\u2019s material and size directly determine resistance: the material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor cross-section is given in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Resistance increases with the span\u2019s length and decreases with cross-section, so long routes or small wires are more expensive to operate. Importantly, these heating losses follow a square law. Because current is proportional to power divided by voltage, the resistive loss on a line grows with the square of the power flow and falls with the square of the operating voltage. Doubling the power on a line results in four times the resistive losses. In this planning model, we convert those physical losses into an operating penalty using {LOSS_COST_RATE}, with the cost driven by the physics relationship that loss is proportional to resistance times the square of the power-to-voltage ratio.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat the network as lossless in terms of nodal balance: power is conserved at each node, and losses are accounted for as a separate financial penalty rather than being subtracted from deliveries. Consumers must receive the required power, and generators must stay within their output limits. Power only moves along lines we construct, and no line may be dispatched above its thermal rating.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost\u2014capital spending on the lines we build plus the expected cost of resistive heating under dispatch. This means we must avoid linear proxies for flow-based costs and instead use a model that captures the square-law behavior of I\u00b2R losses and the mitigating effect of high operating voltage. The resulting plan should identify which connections to build and how to route power so that we meet demand, respect generator and line limits, and achieve the lowest combined construction and operational cost under the specified data inputs."
}